WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:03.880
These are some of the world’s most iconic
typefaces.

00:00:05.320 --> 00:00:11.120
You’ve probably seen them on classic ads,
newspapers, posters, even memes.

00:00:11.130 --> 00:00:13.900
These typefaces were carefully crafted.

00:00:13.900 --> 00:00:15.549
Every curve considered.

00:00:15.549 --> 00:00:17.820
The legibility of their design paramount.

00:00:19.280 --> 00:00:21.040
Oh, wait, I forgot one.

00:00:21.050 --> 00:00:24.010
It’s a little different than the others.

00:00:24.010 --> 00:00:29.320
This one was likely designed on graph paper,
converted into binary code then scanned sixty

00:00:29.320 --> 00:00:31.580
times a second onto a cathode ray screen.

00:00:31.580 --> 00:00:34.679
You probably recognize it when it says this:

00:00:34.679 --> 00:00:37.679
This font had a big responsibility.

00:00:37.679 --> 00:00:42.390
It needed to balance legibility and creativity,
too, but it had to do it while taking up as

00:00:42.390 --> 00:00:46.100
little computer memory as possible.

00:00:50.680 --> 00:00:53.040
If I'm going to write a book, I want to know
everything.

00:00:53.050 --> 00:00:57.319
That’s type designer Toshi Omagari, and
that’s the font for the arcade game Time

00:00:57.319 --> 00:01:02.970
Pilot 84, one of many arcade fonts he catalogued,
analyzed, and reviewed for his book: Arcade

00:01:02.970 --> 00:01:03.960
Game Typography.

00:01:04.560 --> 00:01:07.580
It took months and months of me checking a
hundred games per night.

00:01:07.590 --> 00:01:10.540
There were so many arcade games.

00:01:10.540 --> 00:01:12.720
The book contains around 240 fonts,

00:01:12.720 --> 00:01:17.070
and most of them were designed within this
8x8 grid.

00:01:17.070 --> 00:01:18.690
Why this 8x8 grid?

00:01:18.690 --> 00:01:22.640
Let’s rewind to the golden age of arcade
games.

00:01:22.640 --> 00:01:26.170
Well they come, as if from outer space, in
a variety of weird guises.

00:01:26.170 --> 00:01:28.540
Defender, Pac-Man, Astroids...

00:01:28.540 --> 00:01:32.600
It was space invaders, now all forms of video
arcade games are storming the market.

00:01:32.780 --> 00:01:39.880
Hundreds of circuits and computer chips, a
city of electronic gadgetry and technology.

00:01:39.980 --> 00:01:46.580
Here’s the manual for Sprint 2, an 8-bit
racing game released in 1976, right as arcade

00:01:46.590 --> 00:01:48.120
games were taking over.

00:01:48.120 --> 00:01:52.190
It explains a lot about why early video games
looked the way they did.

00:01:52.840 --> 00:01:58.280
The TV monitor display is divided into a 32
by 28 tile grid.

00:01:58.280 --> 00:02:02.140
Each tile represents one byte of data. That's 8 bits.

00:02:02.580 --> 00:02:06.840
If you zoom in more, you’ll see the 8-bit
tile is divided into its own 8x8 grid where

00:02:06.840 --> 00:02:09.500
each cell can be turned on and off.

00:02:09.960 --> 00:02:14.340
The more cells that are turned on, the more
computer processing power required — which

00:02:14.340 --> 00:02:16.530
was super limited back then.

00:02:16.530 --> 00:02:20.640
The arcade game scanned this information line
by line all the way down the screen with an

00:02:20.640 --> 00:02:24.019
electron beam before it restarted the next
frame.

00:02:24.020 --> 00:02:25.960
This is what it looks like slowed down.

00:02:26.440 --> 00:02:30.780
By today's standards, these tiles would be
a tight constraint, but developers managed

00:02:30.960 --> 00:02:36.660
to make race cars, aliens, spaceships, and robots.

00:02:36.680 --> 00:02:40.660
These games looked the way they did because
they were exercises in efficiency.

00:02:41.200 --> 00:02:45.891
And nothing illustrates that precarious balance
of conserving computer memory more than the

00:02:45.891 --> 00:02:50.670
letters and numbers that guided players through
the game.

00:02:50.670 --> 00:02:55.800
Sprint 2’s set of characters are sort of
like the arcade font’s earliest fossils,

00:02:55.800 --> 00:02:58.569
and they can be traced back to another game
from 1976.

00:02:58.569 --> 00:03:03.769
It all started with a typeface from Atari,
which first showed up in Quiz Show.

00:03:03.769 --> 00:03:07.740
Being a quiz game, I think it needed all the
alphabet letters.

00:03:08.420 --> 00:03:12.020
These characters, like most arcade fonts,
are monospaced.

00:03:12.020 --> 00:03:15.380
As the name suggests, every letter fits within
the same width.

00:03:15.840 --> 00:03:20.300
And because every tile on the screen is locked
together, that 8x8 grid also has to account

00:03:20.310 --> 00:03:22.739
for the gap between characters.

00:03:22.739 --> 00:03:26.840
The Quiz Show character set stands out, first
and foremost, because of its proportions.

00:03:26.840 --> 00:03:32.109
It had two pixels of vertical thickness and
one pixel of horizontal thickness.

00:03:32.109 --> 00:03:35.310
With those rules I would draw an 8 like this.

00:03:35.310 --> 00:03:41.409
But the designers of this font also attempted
to follow the calligraphic tradition of typography.

00:03:41.409 --> 00:03:46.860
So if you hold a flat pen and write an 8 this
way, there will be a thick stress in this

00:03:46.860 --> 00:03:47.720
position.

00:03:48.020 --> 00:03:51.040
This is the Quiz Show “8”

00:03:51.050 --> 00:03:54.540
That stress is also how they differentiated
the zero from the "O".

00:03:54.540 --> 00:03:57.940
So there was a bit of a character in the design.

00:03:57.940 --> 00:04:01.769
The Quiz Show font was designed in the US
and variations of it showed up in a lot of

00:04:01.769 --> 00:04:07.139
Atari games, but it didn’t truly travel
the globe until it jumped over to game developers

00:04:07.139 --> 00:04:10.790
in Japan - who often weren’t familiar with
the Latin alphabet.

00:04:10.790 --> 00:04:15.290
When they started making their own games,
they just used the Atari Quiz Show font.

00:04:15.290 --> 00:04:19.030
Those Japanese developers still couldn’t
resist putting their own spin on it, by adding

00:04:19.030 --> 00:04:22.750
and subtracting a few pixels here and there.

00:04:22.750 --> 00:04:28.620
You might recognize this modified W and Y
from the hit game Galaxian.

00:04:29.980 --> 00:04:35.060
The typeface variations also had their weird
quirks, especially when lower case letters

00:04:35.060 --> 00:04:36.620
entered the picture.

00:04:36.620 --> 00:04:41.460
The biggest challenge was figuring out exactly
what lowercase letters were supposed to look like.

00:04:42.600 --> 00:04:47.080
Case in point - the lowercase characters for
the Japanese-made game Roc ‘N Rope.

00:04:47.080 --> 00:04:50.840
Toshi found them saved within the code of
the game, but they weren’t actually used.

00:04:50.840 --> 00:04:52.340
Probably for the best.

00:04:53.520 --> 00:04:58.380
The a, c, and e are giant compared to the
b and d.

00:04:58.380 --> 00:04:59.800
Some of the letters are in cursive.

00:05:00.180 --> 00:05:03.060
I think this is what they were told in Japan.

00:05:03.060 --> 00:05:07.560
They didn't have as many points of reference when it came to lowercase design.

00:05:07.560 --> 00:05:12.580
Would you say that it's harder to make lower
case letters than upper case letters within

00:05:12.590 --> 00:05:13.770
this system?

00:05:13.770 --> 00:05:20.300
Yes, lowercase needs more space because of
the parts called ascenders and descenders.

00:05:20.300 --> 00:05:25.120
So you also see lowercase g kind of being
pushed up quite a bit.

00:05:27.270 --> 00:05:32.300
I think overall quality is probably best in
Marble Madness.

00:05:32.319 --> 00:05:35.000
That was released by Atari in 1984.

00:05:35.000 --> 00:05:39.560
Marble madness has an R that's more rounded
at the top.

00:05:39.560 --> 00:05:41.560
Because it was also done by Atari,

00:05:42.180 --> 00:05:46.720
it makes me wonder if the original R, this
extra corner, was a mistake or not.

00:05:48.320 --> 00:05:53.139
With incremental advancements in technology
and a lot of ingenuity, arcade game designers

00:05:53.139 --> 00:05:57.060
fit more and more detail into this little
8x8 grid.

00:05:57.060 --> 00:06:01.250
They added more colors, outlines, and shadows.

00:06:01.250 --> 00:06:03.830
The fonts got bolder and more adventurous.

00:06:03.830 --> 00:06:07.440
There were some completely illegible designs
too.

00:06:07.440 --> 00:06:10.870
Sometimes there's a damned if you do, damned
if you don't situation.

00:06:10.870 --> 00:06:15.949
If you put a pixel it looks terrible, but
if you don't, it looks worse.

00:06:15.949 --> 00:06:19.610
Do you have a favorite that you call back
to?

00:06:19.610 --> 00:06:23.980
So, my go to favorite is Sky Fox.

00:06:23.980 --> 00:06:27.039
It’s based on the Copperplate script genre.

00:06:27.039 --> 00:06:31.199
Sky fox has one of the most beautiful script
faces.

00:06:31.199 --> 00:06:33.889
Its use of grey is amazing.

00:06:33.889 --> 00:06:38.090
By using grey It's trying to express the thickness
of one pixel.

00:06:38.090 --> 00:06:41.160
This shouldn't be possible, the entire set
of letters.

00:06:42.380 --> 00:06:47.160
The most fun part for me is the gap
between the game content and the typeface.

00:06:47.180 --> 00:06:51.320
Basically a spaceship takes aim at women in
bikinis that are riding mythical creatures?

00:06:52.060 --> 00:06:57.240
One of the most beautiful typeface, shows up in quite unacceptable games now a days.

00:06:58.180 --> 00:07:04.040
By the end of the arcade’s reign these pixelated
typefaces came in all shapes and sizes.

00:07:04.040 --> 00:07:09.169
But this one stood the test of time, showing
up in hundreds of games including pac-man,

00:07:09.169 --> 00:07:12.400
Donkey Kong, and Legends of Zelda.

00:07:12.400 --> 00:07:16.650
Being confined to this small grid started
as a technical challenge, but quickly became

00:07:16.650 --> 00:07:19.220
an impressive exercise in creativity.

00:07:19.780 --> 00:07:22.440
It’s why this font deserves to be ranked
among some of the greats.

00:07:22.440 --> 00:07:24.600
You can do anything.

00:07:24.720 --> 00:07:28.720
It's a very small grid, but the possibilities
are endless.

